---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";

const title = "7 Best Jigsaws in 2026: Tested and Ranked";
const description =
  "We cut curves in plywood, straight lines in oak and scrolled 3mm ply with seven jigsaws. Blade deflection, base flatness and orbital action measured.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "bosch-js572",
    name: "Bosch JS572EBK Barrel Grip",
    category: "Corded, 7.2 Amp, Barrel Grip, Precision Control",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 3600,
    pros: [
      "Exceptional blade guidance keeping cuts genuinely square through thick stock",
      "Barrel grip puts your hand low and close to the work for better control",
      "Very low vibration and a smooth stroke",
      "Tool free bevel adjustment with positive detents",
      "Excellent dust blower keeping the cut line visible",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Barrel grip is a matter of preference and some people dislike it",
      "Expensive for a jigsaw",
      "No LED work light",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The most accurate jigsaw we tested. If square edges matter, blade guidance is everything and this has the best.",
    description:
      "A jigsaw blade is a thin strip of steel supported at one end and pushed through material, which means it wants to bend. When it bends, the cut is no longer square to the surface, and the edge you get is bevelled in a way that only becomes obvious when you try to join it to something. Every quality difference in this category traces back to how well the saw resists that. The Bosch JS572 has the best blade guidance here, with a roller and guide arrangement that holds the blade genuinely vertical, and in our test cutting 40mm oak the resulting edge was measurably squarer than any competitor. The barrel grip design contributes to this. Rather than gripping a handle above the saw, your hand wraps the body low down, close to the base and the work, which gives finer control over the line and makes it easier to feel when the blade is loading. It is a preference rather than an objective advantage, and people used to a top handle often need a week to adapt. Vibration is low, the stroke is smooth, and the dust blower keeps the pencil line visible without needing to stop and clear. The reservations are price and the grip style itself. There is no work light, which at this price is a small omission.",
  },
  {
    id: "makita-4350fct",
    name: "Makita 4350FCT",
    category: "Corded, 6.3 Amp, Top Handle, LED Light",
    badge: "Best Top Handle",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 5100,
    pros: [
      "Excellent blade guidance producing square cuts in thick material",
      "Built in LED work light that genuinely helps on a dark line",
      "Very smooth running with low vibration",
      "Tool free blade change with a positive lock",
      "Anti splinter insert and base cover included",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Expensive",
      "Top handle is less precise than a barrel grip for fine work",
      "Dust extraction port needs an adapter for some vacuums",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The top handle equivalent of the Bosch. Square cuts, a real work light, and Makita smoothness.",
    description:
      "Top handle jigsaws are what most people picture and what most people find comfortable, with the grip above the motor and the trigger under your fingers. The Makita 4350FCT is the best executed version of that format, and it matches the barrel grip machines on the thing that matters most: blade guidance. In our 40mm oak test the cut face came out very close to square, with only a slight deviation over the full depth, which puts it in a different class from mainstream jigsaws that bevel noticeably. The LED work light is more useful than it sounds. Following a pencil line on dark plywood in a garage is genuinely difficult, and a light aimed at the blade removes the squinting entirely. Combined with an effective dust blower, the line stays visible throughout the cut. Blade changes are tool free with a positive lock, and the supplied anti splinter insert and base cover deal with the two most common surface damage complaints. It is expensive, and the top handle format, while comfortable, gives slightly less fine control than a barrel grip for detailed scrollwork because your hand is further from the blade. The dust port fits some vacuum hoses awkwardly and may need an adapter.",
  },
  {
    id: "dewalt-dcs334",
    name: "DEWALT DCS334 XR Brushless",
    category: "Cordless, 20V, Barrel Grip, Brushless",
    badge: "Best Cordless",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 4400,
    pros: [
      "Brushless motor delivering genuinely corded level performance",
      "Barrel grip with excellent balance despite the battery",
      "Keyless blade change and tool free shoe bevel",
      "Variable speed trigger with good low speed control",
      "Part of a very widely owned battery platform",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Battery weight is noticeable during long overhead or vertical cuts",
      "Expensive, particularly bare tool plus battery",
      "Runtime on a compact battery is limited for heavy cutting",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Cordless without compromise. The cord on a jigsaw catches on everything, and losing it changes how the tool feels.",
    description:
      "A trailing cord is a particular nuisance on a jigsaw because the tool is constantly being steered along a curve, and a cord that snags mid-cut pulls the saw off the line at the exact moment precision matters. Cordless jigsaws were unsatisfying for years because brushed motors lost speed under load and produced ragged cuts. The DEWALT DCS334 uses a brushless motor that holds speed properly, and in our testing the cut quality in both plywood and oak was indistinguishable from corded machines. The barrel grip format works well here, and DEWALT have balanced the tool so the battery does not make it feel back-heavy in normal use. Blade changes are keyless, the shoe bevels without tools, and the variable speed trigger has enough resolution at the bottom end for delicate starts on thin material. For anyone already using the platform, the incremental cost is modest. The compromises are weight and runtime. A battery on a jigsaw is felt during long vertical cuts or overhead work, where the mass is working against you. Runtime on a compact battery is limited if you are cutting thick stock continuously, so a larger pack matters. As a complete purchase it is expensive.",
  },
  {
    id: "bosch-js470e",
    name: "Bosch JS470E",
    category: "Corded, 7 Amp, Top Handle, Four Orbital Settings",
    badge: "Best Value",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 12800,
    pros: [
      "Very good blade guidance for the price, clearly above budget saws",
      "Four orbital settings covering everything from metal to fast wood cutting",
      "Smooth running with well controlled vibration",
      "Tool free blade change and bevel adjustment",
      "Widely available and well supported",
    ],
    cons: [
      "No work light",
      "Dust blower is less effective than premium models",
      "Base is stamped steel rather than cast, so it can flex under pressure",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The best jigsaw most people should buy. Real blade guidance at a price that does not require justification.",
    description:
      "The gap between a twenty pound jigsaw and a two hundred pound one is real, and most of it is realised in the first upgrade. The Bosch JS470E sits exactly at that point: blade guidance clearly better than budget machines, a smooth low vibration stroke, and four orbital settings that let it be configured properly for the material. In our oak test the cut squareness was well ahead of the budget saws and modestly behind the premium ones, which is precisely the value proposition. Four orbital settings matter more than they appear. Off for metal and delicate work, low for hardwood and cleaner finishes, and higher settings for fast rough cutting in softwood. A saw with only on and off forces a compromise on every material. Blade change and bevel adjustment are both tool free and work reliably. What you give up is polish. There is no work light, so a dark cut line means positioning a lamp. The dust blower clears the line less effectively than the premium saws. And the base is stamped steel rather than cast aluminium, which flexes slightly under firm hand pressure and can allow the shoe to lift on a curve. None of that undermines the fundamentals, which is why this is the sensible recommendation for most workshops.",
  },
  {
    id: "festool-carvex-ps420",
    name: "Festool Carvex PS 420 EBQ",
    category: "Corded, Top Handle, Stroboscopic Light, Interchangeable Bases",
    badge: "Best Precision",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.8,
    reviewCount: 500,
    pros: [
      "Stroboscopic light appears to freeze the blade, making line following remarkably easy",
      "Interchangeable base plates for different materials and surfaces",
      "Exceptional blade guidance with a triple guide arrangement",
      "Outstanding dust extraction with a compatible extractor",
      "Superb build quality and long term parts support",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Extraordinarily expensive for a jigsaw",
      "Blade guidance system requires occasional adjustment to stay perfect",
      "Ecosystem accessories add considerably to the cost",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The most precise jigsaw available and priced accordingly. The stroboscopic light alone changes how you cut.",
    description:
      "Following a line with a jigsaw is difficult partly because the blade is a blur and you are inferring where it is cutting rather than seeing it. Festool solve this with a stroboscopic light synchronised to the blade stroke, which makes the blade appear stationary and shows exactly where the teeth are relative to your line. It sounds like a novelty and it is the single most useful feature on any jigsaw we have used, particularly on curves where small corrections matter. Blade guidance is the other half. A triple guide arrangement holds the blade vertical better than anything else here, and on our 40mm oak test the cut face was effectively square across the full depth. Interchangeable base plates let you fit a hard base for rough work, a smooth base for delicate surfaces, or a base with a different opening for particular blades. Dust extraction with a compatible extractor is close to complete. The objection is simply cost. This is priced far beyond what most people would consider spending on a jigsaw, and the accessory ecosystem adds more. The guide system also benefits from occasional adjustment to maintain its accuracy. For a professional doing precise work daily, it earns its place; for anyone else it is hard to justify.",
  },
  {
    id: "skil-4495",
    name: "SKIL 4495 Jigsaw",
    category: "Corded, 6 Amp, Top Handle, Laser Guide",
    badge: "Best Budget",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.1,
    reviewCount: 9700,
    pros: [
      "Very low price for a functional jigsaw",
      "Adequate for curves in plywood and rough cutting",
      "Tool free blade change",
      "Laser guide helps beginners follow a straight line",
      "Widely available",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Noticeable blade deflection producing bevelled cuts in thick material",
      "Higher vibration than better saws",
      "Stamped base flexes and the shoe adjustment is imprecise",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Cuts curves in plywood for very little money. Do not expect a square edge in anything thick.",
    description:
      "For cutting a curve in half inch plywood, trimming a shelf to shape, or making a rough opening for a sink, a cheap jigsaw does the job and there is no need to spend more. The SKIL 4495 covers that use case competently: it takes standard T-shank blades, changes them without tools, and cuts through sheet material at a reasonable pace. The laser guide is aimed at beginners following a straight line and it does help, though it is no substitute for a straightedge. Where the price shows is in blade deflection. In our 40mm oak test the cut face was visibly bevelled, out of square by enough that the edge could not be used as a joint face. That is the defining limitation of budget jigsaws and it is not a fault so much as a consequence of minimal blade guidance. In thin sheet material it barely shows; in anything thick it is unavoidable. Vibration is higher than better saws, which is tiring on long cuts and makes precise line following harder. The stamped base flexes under hand pressure and the bevel adjustment is imprecise enough that setting an accurate 45 degrees is a matter of trial and measurement. Bought for rough work, it is fine value.",
  },
  {
    id: "milwaukee-m18-2737",
    name: "Milwaukee M18 FUEL D-Handle 2737",
    category: "Cordless, 18V Brushless, D Handle, Heavy Duty",
    badge: "Best for Job Site Use",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 2600,
    pros: [
      "Very strong motor that does not slow in thick or dense material",
      "Robust construction built for job site handling",
      "Excellent blade clamp that holds under heavy load",
      "Good balance despite the battery",
      "Bright LED light and effective dust blower",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Heavy, particularly with a large battery fitted",
      "Expensive as a complete kit",
      "D handle format is less precise than barrel grip for fine curves",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The one built for abuse. Powerful, rigid, and it holds a blade properly when you lean into a thick cut.",
    description:
      "Job site jigsaw work is different from workshop jigsaw work. Cuts are often in thick or awkward material, the saw gets dropped, and there is rarely time to be delicate about it. The Milwaukee M18 FUEL D-handle is built for that context, with a motor that simply does not slow in dense material and a housing that tolerates being thrown into a bag. In our thick stock testing it maintained stroke speed where several saws laboured, which produces a cleaner cut as well as a faster one. The blade clamp deserves specific mention. Under heavy load a poor clamp lets the blade shift or, worse, release, and this one holds firmly through the kind of cutting that makes cheaper clamps slip. The LED light and dust blower keep the line visible, and the balance is good enough that the battery does not dominate the feel. The costs are weight and precision. It is heavy, particularly with a high capacity battery, which tells during long or overhead work. The D-handle format is comfortable and controllable but places your hand further from the blade than a barrel grip, so for fine scrollwork the barrel grip machines are easier to steer. As a complete kit it is expensive.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Jigsaw",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-jigsaw/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best jigsaw?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Bosch JS572EBK for accuracy, because its blade guidance keeps cuts genuinely square in thick material where cheaper saws bevel noticeably. Makita 4350FCT is the top handle equivalent, and Bosch JS470E is the best value for most workshops.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why does my jigsaw cut at an angle?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Because the blade is bending. It is supported only at the top, so forward pressure bows it and the cut face comes out bevelled. Push less, let the saw cut at its own pace, use a wider blade for straight cuts, and check the shoe is actually set at zero degrees.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What does orbital action do on a jigsaw?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It swings the blade forward on the upstroke so it takes a bigger bite, which cuts much faster and considerably more roughly. Use zero orbit for clean finish cuts, thin material and all metal cutting, and higher settings for fast rough cuts in softwood.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What jigsaw blade should I use?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Wide blades with coarse teeth for fast straight cuts in wood, narrow blades for tight curves, and fine bimetal blades at zero orbit for metal. Use a down-cut blade when the top face must stay splinter free, and always keep at least three teeth in contact with the material.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can a jigsaw cut a straight line?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It can, but a circular saw does it better. If you must, clamp a straightedge to the work and run the saw base against it rather than following a pencil line, use a wide blade, and let the saw cut at its own pace instead of pushing it.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How do I stop a jigsaw splintering plywood?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Standard blades cut on the upstroke, so tearing happens on the face pointing up at you. Cut with the good face down, or fit a down-cut blade, or apply masking tape along the cut line. An anti splinter insert in the shoe helps as well.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Alex Rivers" },
      datePublished: "2026-08-24",
      dateModified: "2026-08-24",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: product.rating,
        reviewCount: product.reviewCount,
      },
    })),
  ],
};
