          <SocialProof users="6.9k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#blade-guidance" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Why Cheap Saws Cut Crooked</a>
          <a href="#orbital" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. What Orbital Action Does</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Top Picks Reviewed</a>
          <a href="#blades" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Choosing Blades</a>
          <a href="#technique" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Cutting Technique</a>
          <a href="#features" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Features That Matter</a>
          <a href="#uses" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Jigsaw vs Other Saws</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A jigsaw is the tool people buy first and complain about longest, because a cheap one cuts curves acceptably and cuts anything straight or square very badly. The difference is almost entirely blade support: how well the saw stops a thin reciprocating blade from wandering off vertical under load.
        </p>

        <!-- SECTION 1: Why Cheap Jigsaws Cut Crooked -->
        <div id="blade-guidance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Cheap Jigsaws Cut Crooked
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every complaint about jigsaws — bevelled edges, wandering cuts, blades that will not follow a line — comes back to one mechanical problem.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Blade Is Supported at One End Only</h3>

          <p>
            A jigsaw blade is clamped at the top and free at the bottom, and it is being pushed forward through material that resists. That resistance bends the blade backwards and, on a curve, sideways.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bending Produces a Bevelled Cut</h3>

          <p>
            When the blade bows, it is no longer perpendicular to the surface, so the cut face comes out at an angle. On thin sheet material the effect is negligible. In 40mm hardwood a poorly guided saw can be several degrees out.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Guidance Systems Resist the Bending</h3>

          <p>
            Better saws add rollers and guide blocks that grip the blade close to the workpiece, restraining it laterally and fore and aft. That is the mechanical difference between a cheap jigsaw and an expensive one, and it is not visible in a specification list.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Blade Thickness and Width Matter Too</h3>

          <p>
            A thicker, wider blade resists bending better than a thin scrolling blade, which is why straight cuts want a wide blade and tight curves want a narrow one. Trying to make a wide blade turn tightly forces it sideways and produces exactly the wandering people blame on the saw.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Speed and Feed Rate Matter Most of All</h3>

          <p>
            Pushing hard bends the blade more than any other single factor. Letting the saw cut at its own pace, with the base held flat, produces a squarer cut on a cheap saw than forcing a good one.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Jigsaw Fundamentals</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">T-shank</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">The blade fitting to standard on</p>
              </div>
