        <!-- SECTION 2: What Orbital Action Actually Does -->
        <div id="orbital" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Orbital Action Actually Does
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every jigsaw above the very cheapest has an orbital setting, and most people leave it wherever it came set, which wastes the feature entirely.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Orbital action swings the blade forward on the up stroke.</strong> Rather than moving purely up and down, the blade also moves into the cut as it rises, taking a bigger bite. This cuts dramatically faster and considerably more roughly.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Zero orbit gives the cleanest cut.</strong> A purely reciprocating blade cuts slowly and leaves the smoothest edge with the least splintering. This is the setting for finish cuts, thin veneered material, and anything where the edge will be visible.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Metal must be cut at zero orbit.</strong> Orbital action on metal snatches, breaks teeth, and can bend the blade. Metal cutting wants no orbit, a slow speed, and a fine tooth blade with cutting fluid.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Maximum orbit is for rough fast cuts in softwood.</strong> Framing, demolition, and cutting an opening that will be covered. The edge will be ragged and it does not matter.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Tight curves want low or zero orbit.</strong> Orbital action pushes the blade forward, which fights against turning. Reducing the orbit lets the blade follow a curve without being forced sideways.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Set it for every cut, not once.</strong> Thirty seconds spent matching the orbit setting to the material and the cut you are making does more for the result than the difference between two saws.
          </p>
        </div>

