        <!-- SECTION 4: Choosing the Right Blade -->
        <div id="blades" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Choosing the Right Blade
          </h2>

          <p>
            Blade choice affects cut quality more than the saw does, and it is where most people never look.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>T-shank is the standard.</strong> Almost every modern saw takes T-shank blades, which lock positively in tool free clamps. U-shank exists on older saws and the blade selection is far narrower. Buy a T-shank saw.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Tooth count controls speed against smoothness.</strong> Fewer, larger teeth cut fast and rough. More, finer teeth cut slowly and cleanly. Match this to the job rather than using one blade for everything.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Down-cut blades stop splintering on the top face.</strong> Standard blades cut on the upstroke, which tears the surface facing you. A reverse tooth blade cuts downward, giving a clean top face, which matters on laminate and veneered sheet.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Match at least three teeth to the material thickness.</strong> Fewer than three teeth in contact means the blade catches rather than cuts, which is how blades snap and thin metal gets destroyed. Thin material needs fine teeth for this reason.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Narrow blades for tight curves, wide for straight.</strong> A scrolling blade turns tightly and deflects easily. A wide blade holds a straight line and cannot turn. Using one for the other job is the most common blade mistake.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Bimetal blades last far longer.</strong> Carbon steel blades are cheap and dull quickly. Bimetal construction costs more per blade and considerably less per cut, particularly in hardwood and anything containing fasteners.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Turn the Good Face Down</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Standard jigsaw blades cut on the upstroke, so the tearing happens on the surface facing up at you. On veneered plywood, laminate, or anything with a good side, cut with the good face down and the damage happens where it will not be seen. A down-cut blade solves it the other way, at the cost of a rougher underside.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Cutting Accurately -->
        <div id="technique" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Cutting Accurately
          </h2>

          <p>
            The technique that produces square, accurate cuts is not obvious and is mostly about restraint.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Let the saw set the pace.</strong> Pushing forward bends the blade, which bevels the cut. Guide the saw along the line and apply only enough forward pressure to keep it cutting. A saw that is labouring needs a coarser blade or more orbit, not more force.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Keep the base flat and firm on the work.</strong> The base is the reference surface, and any rocking transfers directly to the cut. Firm downward pressure on the base rather than forward pressure on the handle is the correct hand position.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Start the saw before the blade touches.</strong> Bringing a stationary blade into contact and then starting it snatches and can chip the material. Get it up to speed first, then advance into the cut.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Cut outside the line and sand back.</strong> Even a good jigsaw is not a precision instrument. Cutting a millimetre proud and finishing to the line with a sander or file gives a far better result than trying to split the line with the saw.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Use a straightedge clamped to the work for straight cuts.</strong> Running the base against a clamped batten produces a straighter cut than following a pencil line ever will, and it also stops the saw drifting under load.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Support the offcut.</strong> An unsupported offcut sags as the cut nears completion, pinching the blade and splintering the last few inches. Clamping or supporting both sides is a thirty second job that saves the end of every cut.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Features Worth Paying For -->
        <div id="features" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Features Worth Paying For
          </h2>

          <p>
            Jigsaw specification sheets list a great deal that makes no difference and omit the things that do. These are the features that change the tool in use.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Barrel grip versus top handle is a real choice.</strong> A barrel grip puts your hand low and close to the base, which gives finer control on curves. A top handle is more familiar and more comfortable for long cuts. Neither is better; try both if you can.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Tool free blade change is now standard and should be.</strong> A lever that ejects the blade without a hex key removes the friction that stops people changing blades as often as they should. Check it also grips positively, because a slipping clamp under load is dangerous.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>A work light matters more than it sounds.</strong> Following a pencil line on dark plywood in a garage is genuinely hard, and an LED aimed at the blade removes the squinting. Combined with a dust blower it keeps the line visible throughout the cut.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Base quality decides the reference surface.</strong> A cast aluminium base stays flat under hand pressure; a stamped steel one flexes. Since the base is what everything is measured from, that flex translates directly into an inaccurate cut.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Bevel adjustment should have positive detents.</strong> A shoe that clicks into zero and 45 degrees can be reset accurately in seconds. One that slides freely against a printed scale needs measuring every time, and gets left at whatever it was.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Dust extraction is worth the port.</strong> Sawdust obscures the line and jigsaws produce it right where you are looking. A vacuum connection is more effective than any blower, and on a saw with a good shroud it keeps the cut visible throughout.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: What a Jigsaw Is and Is Not For -->
        <div id="uses" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What a Jigsaw Is and Is Not For
          </h2>

          <p>
            A jigsaw is genuinely versatile and it is the wrong tool for several jobs people habitually use it for.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Curves are its purpose.</strong> Nothing else in a home workshop cuts an arbitrary curve in sheet material as easily. Cutting a sink opening, a shaped shelf, or a template is exactly what it exists for.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Plunge cuts are its second talent.</strong> Starting a cut in the middle of a panel, by tipping the saw on the front of its base and lowering the running blade into the material, opens up a whole category of work. Drilling a starter hole is the safer alternative.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>A circular saw is better for straight cuts.</strong> Straighter, faster, squarer and cleaner in sheet material. If the cut is straight and long, reach for the circular saw with a guide rather than fighting a jigsaw along a line.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>A track saw or table saw is better for sheet goods.</strong> Breaking down full sheets of plywood with a jigsaw is slow and produces edges that need work. A track saw does it in one pass with a finished edge.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>A bandsaw is better for repeated curves.</strong> If you cut curves regularly, a bandsaw is faster, more accurate, and leaves a far cleaner edge. The jigsaw's advantage is that it goes to the work rather than the work coming to it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>It cuts metal, tile and plastic with the right blade.</strong> With a fine bimetal blade at zero orbit and low speed it handles sheet metal and pipe. Carbide grit blades cut tile and cement board. This versatility is a genuine reason to own one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 8: Frequently Asked Questions -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best jigsaw?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Bosch JS572EBK for accuracy, because its blade guidance keeps cuts genuinely square in thick material where cheaper saws bevel noticeably. Makita 4350FCT is the top handle equivalent, and Bosch JS470E is the best value for most workshops.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does it cut crooked?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Because the blade is bending. It is supported only at the top, so forward pressure bows it and the cut face comes out bevelled. Push less, let the saw cut at its own pace, use a wider blade for straight cuts, and check the shoe is actually set at zero degrees.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is orbital action?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It swings the blade forward on the upstroke so it takes a bigger bite, which cuts much faster and considerably more roughly. Use zero orbit for clean finish cuts, thin material and all metal cutting, and higher settings for fast rough cuts in softwood.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which blade should I use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Wide blades with coarse teeth for fast straight cuts in wood, narrow blades for tight curves, and fine bimetal blades at zero orbit for metal. Use a down-cut blade when the top face must stay splinter free, and always keep at least three teeth in contact with the material.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can it cut straight?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can, but a circular saw does it better. If you must, clamp a straightedge to the work and run the saw base against it rather than following a pencil line, use a wide blade, and let the saw cut at its own pace instead of pushing it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I stop splintering?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Standard blades cut on the upstroke, so tearing happens on the face pointing up at you. Cut with the good face down, or fit a down-cut blade, or apply masking tape along the cut line. An anti splinter insert in the shoe helps as well.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Jigsaw Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every complaint about jigsaw cut quality comes from one of these four habits rather than from the saw.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pushing the Saw Through the Cut</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Forward pressure bows the blade backwards and sideways, which is exactly what produces the bevelled, wandering cuts people blame on the tool. Apply firm downward pressure on the base to keep it flat, guide the saw along the line, and let the blade cut at whatever pace it wants.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using One Blade for Everything</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A wide blade will not turn a tight curve and a narrow scrolling blade will not hold a straight line. Coarse teeth tear thin material and fine teeth clog in thick softwood. Blades cost very little compared with a ruined workpiece, and matching one to the job is the single cheapest quality improvement available.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving Orbital Action Set Wherever It Was</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Maximum orbit in thin veneered ply destroys the edge; zero orbit in framing timber is needlessly slow. Orbit on metal snatches and breaks teeth. Setting it deliberately for each material and cut takes seconds and changes the result more than the choice of saw does.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cutting on the Line Rather Than Beside It</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A jigsaw is not a precision instrument even at its best. Cutting a millimetre outside the line and finishing to it with a sander, file or router gives a far more accurate result than trying to split the line with a reciprocating blade that is inevitably deflecting slightly.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

